## Results
**Part A (tree, 2 features).** Manual and scikit-learn trees give identical predictions (Gini, depth 4: 13 leaves, train 0.943 / test 0.934, 100 % agreement). Training accuracy goes to 1.0 as depth grows but test accuracy peaks at ~0.94 around depth 3–6 and then falls (0.931 unrestricted, 71 leaves) → over-fitting. Gini and entropy behave almost the same.

**Part B (linear SVM, 2 features).** Manual solution ≈ scikit-learn (same objective value, weight cosine similarity 0.9998, identical test predictions, 0.887 accuracy). The two features are not linearly separable, so ~31 % of training points violate the margin. Small `C` → wide margin (3.4 at C=0.001, ~0.95 for C≥10); accuracy barely changes. Learning rates ≤ 0.001 do not converge in 1000 iterations, ≥ 0.1 do; the objective gap shrinks steadily with iterations while test accuracy stabilises after ~10.

**Part C (kernels).** Linear kernel: 0.87 on moons and 0.48 (chance) on circles. Polynomial / RBF: 0.91 / 0.92 on moons and 0.99 / 0.99 on circles. A nonlinear kernel implicitly maps the data to a space where a linear separator exists; `gamma` controls under- vs over-fitting.

**Part D (all 4 features, same split).**

| Model | Train acc | Test acc | Precision | Recall | F1 | Fit (ms) | Predict (ms) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Decision tree – manual (depth 6) | 0.998 | 0.989 | 0.976 | 1.000 | 0.988 | ≈5 | ≈0.06 |
| Decision tree – sklearn | 0.998 | 0.989 | 0.976 | 1.000 | 0.988 | ≈2 | ≈0.08 |
| Linear SVM – manual | 0.985 | 0.982 | 0.961 | 1.000 | 0.980 | ≈176 | ≈0.01 |
| Linear SVM – sklearn | 0.985 | 0.982 | 0.961 | 1.000 | 0.980 | ≈7 | ≈0.3 |
| RBF SVM – sklearn (C=1, γ=0.5) | 1.000 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 1.000 | 1.000 | ≈4 | ≈0.7 |

(Timings vary slightly from run to run; exact values are in `results/D_final_comparison.csv`.)

## Observations
- **Features matter most:** 2 features cap tree/linear models at ~0.89–0.94; with all 4 features every model is ≥ 0.98.
- **Implementations agree:** the manual tree equals scikit-learn's; the manual SVM converges to scikit-learn's weights given enough iterations (it needed `lr=1.0`, 5000 iterations on 4 features) but is ~25× slower to train.
- **Interpretability:** tree (readable if/else rules) > linear SVM (5 signed weights, on scaled inputs) > RBF SVM (83 support vectors, black box).
- **Scaling:** trees are scale-invariant (identical predictions); SVMs need training-set scaling. Here features have similar magnitudes, so the measured effect was small — it matters much more with mixed units.
- **Overfitting / complexity:** unrestricted tree → 100 % train accuracy; CV picked depth 6. Linear SVM has the smallest train–test gap. RBF SVM has the highest capacity but was tuned by cross-validation on the training set only.
- **Prediction cost:** tree and linear SVM are cheapest; RBF SVM grows with the number of support vectors.
- **Caveats:** the test set has only 274 rows (1 error ≈ 0.4 points), so tree vs linear SVM differences are a few samples. The dataset has 24 duplicate rows (9 test rows duplicate a training row); excluding them does not change the ranking. All errors are false positives (genuine flagged as forged) — recall is 1.0 for every model.

